// Multi-service scenario-driving simulation runner. Generated by CIaC
// (28UpdatePlan.md M7c) -- the `system-runner` module's own
// `main.go`, one per multi-service system, generalizing
// `sim_runner.go.j2`'s single-service interpreter across every
// service this system declares.
//
// Depends on `sim-shared` and every service's own Go module (all
// resolved through `go.mod` `replace` directives pointing at `../
// <dir>`), importing each service's `simbridge` package -- found live
// (`go build`'s own "use of internal package ... not allowed"
// refusal): Go's directory-based `internal/` visibility rule blocks a
// *separate* module from importing anything under a service's own
// `internal/` tree at all (config, state, routes, workers, schemas
// included, not only `world`), regardless of the `replace` directive
// that makes the module itself resolvable. `simbridge` is each
// service's own thin, non-`internal` re-export of exactly what this
// runner needs (see `templates/simbridge.go.j2`'s own doc comment);
// every service's own package is imported under one alias (its
// PascalCase module suffix) since `simbridge` is otherwise the same
// base name for every service. One shared `sim-shared/world.World`
// (the same nominal type every service's own `state.go` imports, per
// M7c's `sim-shared` module) is constructed once and threaded into
// every service's own `NewSimulation`.
//
// Unlike TypeScript's system-runner (which inlines one `app.inject()`
// call per (service, api) pair to sidestep Fastify's own per-app
// generic-variance friction) or Rust's (which needs a synchronous
// `block_on_ready` bridge), Go's `*http.ServeMux` is a plain, non-
// generic concrete type -- every service's own router has the exact
// same static type, so one shared `doInject` helper drives every
// (service, api) pair uniformly, and `world.RegisterAPI`'s handler
// type (`func(req any) (any, error)`) is already synchronous, needing
// no bridge at all.
//
// Deliberately narrow, matching every other generated runner's own
// disclosed scope: `publish` steps are not wired; `drain`/`expect.
// quiescence` see one global undelivered-message buffer, not
// independent per-`(subject, group)` cursors across services. Any
// scenario step this runner cannot honor is a hard error, never a
// silently-skipped no-op.
package main

